<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.TypeSystemOverview
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/bml https://blockml.org/schemas/bml.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:bml="org.blockml.bml.annotation.BmlAnnotation"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:type="org.blockml.bml.type">

  <name>TypeSystemOverview</name>
  <type>doc:TypeSystemOverview</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A guide to the type system</is>

  <documentation>
    BlockML knows four ValueType kinds at language level — each names a semantic meaning.
    They are sibling branches under ValueType (not subtypes of each other):

    PrimitiveType — atomic scalar semantics; no stored values on the type itself.
    Examples: Boolean, Integer, Decimal, Float, Text, Color (Text subtype), URL, UUID, Version,
    TypeIdentifier / TypeReference, PackageIdentifier / PackageReference, InstanceIdentifier /
    InstanceReference, MemberIdentifier / PropertyIdentifier / …, Date, Time, DateTime, Duration.

    StructType — custom value objects; structure via properties.
    Examples: EmptyStruct (no fields; only {}), Size3D, Address, Transform. Parameterized
    types (Box(T)) are StructTypes with genericTypes — not a separate branch.

    EnumType — closed set of named members; own ValueType branch (not a StructType). Allowed
    members live in the values aggregation; BOM stores id + typeFqn only.
    Examples: Direction, Face, StudType.

    ConstantType — singleton value domain; one configured literal.
    Example: Constant(Text){value: "auto"} for union alternatives.

    Type expressions combine named types — they are not new BlockML type classes:
    • Multiplicity: Integer[], Integer[3], Text[0..n] — postfix on a TypeExpression, so it nests via
      explicit grouping: (Integer[])[] (never Integer[][])
    • Unions: Boolean | Integer, Constant(Text){value: "auto"} | Float
    • Generic application: Box(Integer) · InstanceReference(core:Block) — round parentheses in type
      attributes; open generic instances bind the same (…) list via bml:generics (doc:GenericsModel)
    • Instance configuration: Text{maxLength: 100}, Decimal{precision: 10, scale: 2}
    • bml:Self — resolves to effective current Block type after inheritance
    • T — generic parameter reference from declaring Block's genericTypes

    Assignability follows the branch hierarchy — a value of a subtype is accepted wherever a
    supertype is required, with ValueType as the top of the value hierarchy:
    Integer &lt;: Number &lt;: PrimitiveType &lt;: ValueType. Date &lt;: Temporal &lt;:
    PrimitiveType. Duration is a sibling of Temporal under PrimitiveType — not a Temporal subtype.
    A type reference with multiplicity is compatible per element type: Integer[] &lt;: Number[]
    &lt;: ValueType[], and each element of a collection is validated independently, so ValueType[]
    also accepts mixed element types (doc:TypeExpressions — Collection compatibility · Heterogeneous
    collections).

    Only ValueTypes may appear where a bare type name is required (Property type,
    Capability inputType/outputType). Aggregation type may be any Block FQN. Association type
    must be InstanceReference(T) or InstanceReference(T)[] — bare Block FQNs are invalid.
    ValueTypes never store data — Properties own all concrete values.

    Identifier vs Reference — the validator keys off the declared ValueType of the field, never
    the property or attribute name (type= vs abc= is irrelevant):
    • TypeReference, MemberReference (PropertyReference / AggregationReference / …), and
      InstanceReference — the target must resolve in the current corpus. Unknown TypeReference
      → BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE; unknown MemberReference → BML_UNKNOWN_PROPERTY (or BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE
      if the owning type cannot be resolved); unknown InstanceReference →
      BML_UNRESOLVABLE_INSTANCE_REF. Unresolved is an error, not proof the document is
      ill-formed — like Java class-not-found, it may be a typo or a missing library.
    • TypeIdentifier, MemberIdentifier (PropertyIdentifier / …), and InstanceIdentifier —
      syntax only. Ill-formed → BML_INVALID_TYPE_REFERENCE. An unknown but well-formed name is
      valid.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see type="doc:TypeExpressions" relation="AuthoringGuide">
      Multiplicity, unions, obsolete wrappers — type expressions.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:GenericsModel" relation="AuthoringGuide">
      Generic declarations, inheritance binding, instance bml:generics, defaultType.
    </see>
    <see type="core:ValueType" relation="Extends">
      Abstract base for all value blocks used in Property and Capability types.
    </see>
    <see type="type:PrimitiveType" relation="SeeAlso">
      Branch base for atomic scalar types.
    </see>
    <see type="type:StructType" relation="SeeAlso">
      Branch base for custom value objects.
    </see>
    <see type="type:EmptyStruct" relation="SeeAlso">
      Instantiable StructType with no fields — only {}.
    </see>
    <see type="type:EnumType" relation="SeeAlso">
      Branch base for finite value sets — sibling of StructType under ValueType.
    </see>
    <see type="type:ConstantType" relation="SeeAlso">
      Branch base for singleton value domains.
    </see>
    <see type="type:Constant" relation="SeeAlso">
      Generic Constant(T){value: …} for union singleton alternatives.
    </see>
    <see type="type:InstanceReference" relation="SeeAlso">
      Association member types — instance pointers, not owned Block instances.
    </see>
    <see type="type:Color" relation="SeeAlso">
      Textual primitive subtype — not a StructType.
    </see>
    <see type="type:Decimal" relation="SeeAlso">
      Default primitive for non-integer numeric values.
    </see>
    <see type="type:Temporal" relation="SeeAlso">
      Temporal position branch — Date, Time, DateTime.
    </see>
    <see type="type:Duration" relation="SeeAlso">
      Elapsed-time magnitude — not a Temporal subtype.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:TypeExpressions" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — multiplicity, unions, and type-expression forms.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:ContractsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
      contracts merge and satisfiesTypeBound — baseType vs implements.
    </see>
  </references>

</block>
